❯ Let's say i have a fixed set of memory allocations where the sizes and order of allocation and deallocation are known ahead of time. Is there a known algorithm to pack them that minimizes the total memory high-water makr?

Yes. This is the offline dynamic storage allocation (DSA) problem, sometimes called interval packing or weighted interval coloring. Each buffer is a rectangle with a fixed horizontal extent (its lifetime) and a height (its size). You choose a vertical offset for each one so that live rectangles never overlap, and you minimize the total height. That height is your high-water mark.

Key facts about the problem

- It is NP-hard. Stockmeyer showed this holds even when every size is 1 or 2. It appears as problem SR2 in Garey and Johnson. So there is no known efficient exact algorithm in general.
- The obvious lower bound is the max load. That is the peak over time of the sum of all live sizes. The optimum is often equal to it, but not always. Fragmentation forced by the lifetime structure can push the optimum a small constant factor above it.
- Unit sizes are easy. If all buffers are the same size, the interval graph is perfect. Sorting by start time and assigning greedily achieves exactly the max load. This is why the hardness comes entirely from mixed sizes.

Approximation results

- Kierstead proved First Fit by start time is a constant-factor approximation (a 6-approximation for the weighted case).
- Gergov (1999) gave a 3-approximation.
- Buchsbaum, Karloff, Kenyon, Reingold, and Thorup (2003) gave a (2+ε)-approximation, and showed the optimum approaches the max load when every buffer is small relative to the peak. That regime matches most real workloads.

What practitioners actually use

- Greedy heuristics. Sort buffers by size descending (or by lifetime length) and place each at the lowest offset that fits given already-placed overlapping buffers. This is what XLA's heap simulator does (GlobalDecreasingSizeBestFitHeap) and what the TFLite memory planner does, described in Pisarchyk and Lee, "Efficient Memory Management for Deep Neural Net Inference" (2020). Greedy-by-size usually gets within a few percent of the lower bound and often hits it.
- Exact solvers for moderate instance sizes. Since the instance is fully known offline, you can formulate it as an ILP or constraint program and solve it exactly for up to a few thousand buffers. Google published two solvers tuned for this: TelaMalloc (Maas et al., ASPLOS 2023) and MiniMalloc (Moffitt, ASPLOS 2024). MiniMalloc is open source and solves XLA-sized instances in seconds using branch and bound with strong dominance pruning. It is probably the best starting point if you want optimal rather than heuristic.
- Hybrid. Run greedy first. If it hits the max-load lower bound, you are done and provably optimal. Otherwise hand the instance to an exact solver with the greedy result as an upper bound.

Two practical caveats

- Alignment and allocation granularity change the sizes, so compute the lower bound on the rounded sizes, not the nominal ones.
- If your sizes are all powers of two or otherwise nicely nested, buddy-style placement can also reach the lower bound without a solver, since nested sizes avoid the awkward fragmentation that makes the general problem hard.



