▎ You: Is it possible to create a custom comparator for an std::set such that every element in the set knows its position in the overall sorted order?

▎ Claude: No. A comparator can't do it, and the limitation is structural rather than something cleverness in the comparator can work around.
▎
▎ Why a comparator can't help
▎
▎ - The comparator only ever sees two elements at a time. It never sees the tree, the subtree sizes, or where the insertion landed.
▎ - Positions are not a local property. Inserting one element shifts the rank of every element after it, so "each element knows its position" implies O(n) work per insert or erase no matter who does it.
▎ - std::set is a red-black tree whose nodes don't store subtree sizes, so even the container itself can't answer "what is the rank of this element" faster than std::distance(s.begin(), it), which is O(n).
▎
▎ What you actually want is an order-statistic tree
▎
▎ If you're on GCC or Clang with libstdc++, the policy-based data structures extension gives you exactly this with O(log n) rank queries:
▎
▎ #include <ext/pb_ds/assoc_container.hpp>
▎ #include <ext/pb_ds/tree_policy.hpp>
▎ using namespace __gnu_pbds;
▎
▎ template <class T, class Cmp = std::less<T>>
▎ using ordered_set = tree<T, null_type, Cmp, rb_tree_tag,
▎                          tree_order_statistics_node_update>;
▎
▎ ordered_set<int> s;
▎ s.insert(10); s.insert(20); s.insert(30);
▎ s.order_of_key(20);   // 1  : number of elements strictly less than 20
▎ *s.find_by_order(2);  // 30 : element at rank 2
▎
▎ Note that tree with rb_tree_tag rejects duplicate keys. For a multiset, use pairs of (value, unique_id) as the key.
▎
▎ Other options depending on your workload
▎
▎ - Portable implementation. Write a treap or AVL tree augmented with subtree sizes. Rank and select are both O(log n). This is standard and not much code.
▎ - Mostly reads, rare writes. Keep a sorted std::vector. Rank is lower_bound minus begin, O(log n). Inserts are O(n) but with a tiny constant, which often beats a tree in practice.
▎ - Known key universe. Coordinate-compress the keys and use a Fenwick tree of counts. Rank is a prefix sum, O(log n), and it handles duplicates naturally.
▎ - Boost. boost::intrusive containers and boost::multi_index support ranked or random-access indices on top of ordered sets.
▎
▎ If you tell me what your access pattern looks like, I can suggest which of these fits best.

