▎ You: Is it possible to make an efficient caching allocator where malloc returns the contiguous block of memory at the lowest address that's big enough to satisfy the requested size?

▎ Claude: Yes. The classic objection to address-ordered first fit is that a linear scan of the free list is O(n), but the search is really a "leftmost element with size ≥ k" query, and that is O(log n) with the right data structure. Several production allocators do exactly this.
▎
▎ The standard trick: an address-keyed tree augmented with subtree max size. Keep free blocks in a balanced tree ordered by address. Each node also stores the largest free block size anywhere in its subtree. The search descends from the root:
▎
▎ find_lowest_fit(node, k):
▎     if node.left and node.left.max_size >= k:  go left
▎     elif node.size >= k:                        return node
▎     else:                                       go right   # right.max_size must be >= k, or no fit exists
▎
▎ Because you always prefer left when the left subtree can satisfy the request, the first node you stop at is the lowest address that fits. Insert, delete, split, and coalesce are all O(log n). Coalescing is easy because address order gives you the neighbors directly as predecessor and successor, so you don't even need boundary tags. The augmentation is maintained on rotations the same way a segment tree or order-statistics tree maintains its summaries.
▎
▎ Linux uses precisely this for the kernel virtual address range: mm/vmalloc.c keeps free vmap areas in an rbtree augmented with subtree_max_size, and find_vmap_lowest_match is the descent above. Stephenson's "Fast Fits" (1983) is the older version of the same idea using a Cartesian tree, keyed by address with heap order on size.
▎
▎ If block granularity is fixed, a segment tree over a bitmap is even better. For a caching allocator that rounds to a page or chunk size, represent each segment as an array of chunks and build a segment tree where each node stores the longest free run inside it, plus the free run touching its left edge and its right edge. Merging two children is three comparisons. Lowest-address fit is again a left-preferring descent in O(log P). This is contiguous, cache-friendly, and has no per-block headers, which matters when the memory you're managing is on a device you can't put metadata in.
▎
▎ Trade-offs worth knowing before switching from best fit.
▎
▎ - Address-ordered first fit tends to create a litter of small splinters at the low end as big blocks get split from the front. Every search skips them in O(log n) so it's not a time problem, but they are fragmentation.
▎ - The usual reason to want it is that it concentrates live data at low addresses, leaving the high end of each segment empty and therefore releasable. That works best when you also order segments by something sensible, since "lowest address" across independently allocated segments is an arbitrary total order. Ordering by segment base address is fine; ordering by segment age or fullness is sometimes better for trimming.
▎ - In Wilson et al.'s 1995 survey, address-ordered first fit and best fit had roughly the same, low, fragmentation on real workloads, and both beat LIFO or size-ordered first fit. So you're paying similar per-op cost for a different placement bias, not a strictly better one.
▎ - A caching allocator with per-stream pools needs one tree per pool, or a stream component as the primary key. Cross-stream address order is meaningless because blocks aren't interchangeable across streams anyway.
▎
▎ If you want a cheap approximation instead of exact lowest address, jemalloc's approach is to bin free extents by size class and pick the lowest address within the smallest class that fits. That gives most of the locality benefit with simpler bookkeeping, but it is not globally lowest-address.
